global society to make  sure that we have an answer  so that comet doesn't hit Earth and so  that we rebalance our relationship with  Mother Earth in a way that everybody can  have a decent life based on our values  on this planet that ladies and gentlemen  is the basis for the Green Deal this is  not just an answer to climate change it  is an answer to the many fold changes  that we are facing it is also a growth  strategy if we implement the policies  that we advocate in the Green Deal it  means rebooting our economy setting it  on a completely different footing  looking completely differently and how  we produce food and consume food looking  completely differently how we organize  mobility how we negotiate our  relationship with other parts of the  world how we create a better balance  between our cities and our rural areas  etc etc so we can only is it still not  working they can't in the outside would  want that to happen so what I'm trying  to say is this it is much more than us  trying to attain the Peris objectives it  is about understanding that we need to  be masters of our own fate and we can  only do that if we if we mobilize  governance at all levels local regional  national European  global and of all nature not no longer  just governance by governments but  governments governance by civil society  by everyone whose interest in this by  economic actors by trade unions by NGOs  by the scientific community by academia  allowed by the media etc that we all  understand we have a stake in this that  we do this in a way that is  intergenerational because I fear a lack  of understanding between generations if  we're not very careful and all of that  you can read in essence in the Green  Deal and I will be happy to go into all  the different elements of the Green Deal  but it is a comprehensive approach to  the issue it's not a blueprint it's not  a blueprint it's a road map  it offers avenues for us starting with  the assumption that we want to be or the  hopefully the commitment in law at some  point soon that we will be Europe a  climate neutral continent by 2050 and  then starting from that conclusion take  steps back until today and see what  needs to be done in intermediary steps  to get there and obviously one of the  first things we will need to do is make  a sound assessment of where we need to  be in 2030 and you know we have a lot of  scientific indications but I want to do  a very thorough impact assessment  between now and somewhere in the summer  next year so that we know exactly what  needs to be done to get us where we need  to be what I find most exciting and also  stimulating is that society is caught on  to the urgency of the matter over 90% of  Europeans see climate change as an  urgent matter that needs to be dealt  with and this is the best starting point  you can have as a politician if there's  so much support for taking measures in  such an area I also see but we will hear  a bit more  about this later on I also see a sea  change in the attitude of the private  sector only in a very few years well he  was on it for a longer time but most of  them only a very few years with a few  exceptions a few exceptions especially  in the fossil fuel industry but mostly  especially big corporations are on the  same line already understanding that  this paradigm shift is something they  have to embrace rather than try and slow  down the challenge in the private sector  ladies and gentlemen I think we'll be  with small and medium sized enterprises  because for them they don't do planning  over 20 30 or 40 years they think about  tomorrow or next year and how they keep  their employees employed now and we need  to make sure that we have a proposition  for them for instance making it easier  for them to refurbish housing to make  sure houses are isolated and our are  made a climate neutral or are fitted  with sustainable energy sources that  could be a huge engine for jobs and it  can be done the capital is there the  it's it's a matter of governance of  organizing it of mobilizing banks of  mobilizing intermediary agencies such as  chambers of commerce of creating nexuses  with bigger industries so that they  understand they need to take the smaller  medium-sized enterprises along on this  road and I think if we can show some  early successes if you can show this  works we can take away a lot of the fear  that people have for this change because  we live in a day and age and let me end  on that we live in a day and age where  for the first time in a long time  the dominant feeling is not my kids will  be better off than I was so let's put  our shoulders on them the dominant  feeling is I fear for the future of my  kids they will probably be worse off  than I am so what is it if that is the  dominant feeling what will you do what  will your attitude be if then a  politician comes along and tells you we  to change you will say change equals  loss and I don't want that so no change  and if we don't break that mold we will  be in the ironic or a really sad  situation that those people who will  suffer most if we don't address the  challenge of climate change the most  vulnerable people in our society can be  mobilized to be also the most vocal  people to stop us responding to the  challenge of climate change because they  believe there's nothing in it for them  other than loss so if we want this to  happen we have to make sure it is done  in a socially viable way so that people  see not just here but see that we leave  no one behind and then I believe we can  create we can mobilize societies to such  an extent that it will embrace the  opportunities that this new way of  looking at our society has to offer that  ladies and gentlemen is hopefully the  essence of the Green Deal when you look  at it more carefully and again it will  only happen you know Europe is  responsible for approximately 9% of  emissions so it only will be effective  on a global scale if we can convince our  other partners to take comparable  measures or to go along the same path  and we will do everything we can all our  efforts will be directed at that and  that's why we're here and that's why  we're working hard at this cop but by  the cop next year in Glasgow we have to  be ready we have to have our plans  worked out we have to be able to make a  proposition to the other parts of the  world that has a realistic perspective  of keeping us on track to fulfill the  Paris goals thank you very much for your  attention  [Applause]
 

